   <TD VALIGN=TOP COLSPAN=2>
   
	
<FONT SIZE=-1><FONT COLOR="#D60021">3.</FONT> <A HREF="../ppc.htm"><FONT COLOR="#00105A"> Production Process Characterization                                                                                                                                                                                                                          </a></FONT></FONT><BR>
<BR>
<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">3.2.</FONT>  Assumptions / Prerequisites                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   </H2>
<FONT SIZE=-1>
</P>
</FONT>
   </TD>
   </TR>

   
<TD WIDTH=15%></TD>


<TD WIDTH=85%>The techniques of production process characterization make certain assumptions about the kinds of problems to which they will be applied.  There is also a certain amount prerequisite knowledge that the reader of this chapter is assumed to posses.  This section gives an overview of those assumptions and prerequisites.  Details on many of these prerequisites are given in other chapters of this book.</td></tr>
<TR><TD WIDTH=15%><i>click on the desired topic to read more</I></TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<OL>
<OL>
<LI><A HREF="ppc210.htm">How do I model production processes?</A>
<OL>
<LI><A HREF="ppc211.htm">Process Variability</A>
<LI><A HREF="ppc212.htm">Factors and Responses</A>
<LI><A HREF="ppc213.htm">Models for Relating Factors and Responses</A>
</OL>
<LI><A HREF="ppc220.htm">What about Measurement Error?</A>
<OL>
<LI><A HREF="ppc221.htm">The Effects of Metrology Error</A>
<LI><A HREF="ppc222.htm">Conducting Gauge Studies to Estimate Measurement 
    Error</A>
<LI><A HREF="ppc223.htm">Models for Accumulating Error</A>
</OL>
</OL>
</OL>
</TD>
</TR>
   
   </TABLE>
   </UL>
